NAME
     Objectworks for Smalltalk-80

SYNOPSIS
     st80 [ _o_p_t_i_o_n_s ] st80.im
     st80 [ _o_p_t_i_o_n_s ] _l_o_c_a_l_I_m_a_g_e_N_a_m_e

DESCRIPTION
     st80 takes the virtual image file name as its argument; this
     may  be the standard Smalltalk-80 system virtual image (usu-
     ally /usr/smalltalk/image/st80.im or simply, st80.im ), or a
     customized  snapshot  version  of the virtual image.  It may
     also take options (see below).

OPTIONS
     -a _n_u_m_b_e_r
               Address space warning.  If set, a low space notif-
          ier  appears  when  the  address  space grows by _n_u_m_b_e_r
          bytes.  Useful to keep tabs on space consumption.

     -g _n_u_m_b_e_r
               The maximum amount of time allowed  for  automatic
          garbage  collection  (expressed  as a percentage of the
          total time of the computation).  For example,  with  -g
          10,  if  the  last  garbage  collection took 2 seconds,
          another garbage collection would  not  start  until  at
          least 20 seconds had elapsed and space was getting low.
          Or, -g 0 suppresses the automatic  mark-and-sweep  gar-
          bage collector entirely.  The -g switch does not affact
          the primary storage reclamation  system,  a  generation
          scavenger,  which  should  be  invisible  to the system
          user.

     -h _n_u_m_b_e_r
               Height.  Sets the height of the  Smalltalk  window
          when  the system comes up (in pixels).  By default, the
          Smalltalk window will be the full size of the display.

     -l _n_u_m_b_e_r
               Size of the large-object zone.  If set,  the  size
          of  the  zone in which large-objects are housed will be
          _n_u_m_b_e_r  bytes.   The  default  large-object  zone  size
          varies from platform to platform (typically hundreds of
          KB).

     -m _n_u_m_b_e_r
               Total space allocated to object memory (in bytes).
          If  you use this switch, you will be given exactly this
          amount of space at startup time and no more thereafter.
          The  -m  switch  disables  the  default  mechanism that
          starts the system with an object memory large enough to
          hold  the  image  and  then  increases object memory as



